# OWASP Top 10

## OWASP Top 10:2025 Overview

OWASP just released the Top 10:2025, replacing the 2021 list. Worth knowing both versions since a lot of study material still references 2021. The shift overall is less "individual coding mistake," more "systemic failure in how apps get built and operated."

| Rank | 2025 | 2021 (for comparison) |
|---|---|---|
| A01 | Broken Access Control | Broken Access Control (same, #1 both versions) |
| A02 | Security Misconfiguration | Cryptographic Failures |
| A03 | Software Supply Chain Failures | Injection |
| A04 | Cryptographic Failures | Insecure Design |
| A05 | Injection | Security Misconfiguration |
| A06 | Insecure Design | Vulnerable and Outdated Components |
| A07 | Authentication Failures | Identification and Authentication Failures |
| A08 | Software or Data Integrity Failures | Software and Data Integrity Failures |
| A09 | Security Logging and Alerting Failures | Security Logging and Monitoring Failures |
| A10 | Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions | Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) |

### What's genuinely new

Software Supply Chain Failures (A03): promoted out of "Vulnerable and Outdated Components" into its own category. Reflects how much real damage comes from compromised dependencies, think SolarWinds-style attacks.

Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions (A10): brand new category. Covers apps that fail unsafely, error handling that leaks sensitive info, or exception paths that skip security checks entirely.

SSRF got folded out as a standalone entry, now mostly covered under Insecure Design or Broken Access Control depending on the specific case.

